The importance of having an ASCII code across all machines lies in ensuring compatibility and uniformity in character representation. ASCII (American Standard Code for Information Interchange) assigns a unique binary code to each character, allowing computers of different brands, specifications, and sizes to communicate and work together seamlessly. This standardization was approved in 1963 and has been revised multiple times to accommodate evolving needs. Although ASCII's 128-character limit does not cover all written languages, it remains backward compatible and forms the foundation for newer encoding standards like Unicode, which integrate ASCII as the first 128 codes.

This standardization ensures that when data is transferred or displayed across different systems, characters are interpreted consistently, preventing miscommunication or data corruption.

